      "title": "Vector Spaces: Overview",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.667,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Introduction to vector spaces for a first course in linear algebra",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Topics: vectors in R^n, vector space axioms, subspaces",
          "score": 0.8,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Also: span, linear independence and bases",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Closes with a short exercise and its solution",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 2,
      "title": "Vectors in R^n",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.575,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "A vector in R^n is an ordered list of n real numbers: u = (u1, ..., un)",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Addition is done component by component",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Scalar multiplication multiplies every component by a real number",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The zero vector has every component equal to zero",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Vectors in R^2 and R^3 can be drawn as arrows from the origin",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "equation",
          "text": "Two vectors are added by adding their corresponding components.",
          "score": 0.6,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "equation",
          "text": "Multiplying a vector by a scalar a multiplies each of its components by a.",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 3,
      "title": "Vector Space Axioms",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.553,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "A real vector space is a set V with addition and scalar multiplication satisfying axioms",
          "score": 0.889,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Addition is commutative and associative",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "There is a zero vector; every vector has an additive inverse",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Scalar multiplication distributes over vector addition and over scalar addition",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "equation",
          "text": "Scaling a sum of vectors gives the same result as summing the scaled vectors.",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 4,
      "title": "Examples and Why Axioms Matter",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.707,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "R^n is a vector space",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Polynomials of degree at most n form a vector space",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "All m by n matrices form a vector space",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Any theorem proved from the axioms holds in all these examples at once",
          "score": 0.833,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 5,
      "title": "Subspaces",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.659,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "W ⊆ V is a subspace if it is a vector space with the same operations",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Check: W contains the zero vector",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Check: W is closed under addition and under scalar multiplication",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Example: the line y = 2x in R^2 is a subspace",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Non-example: y = 2x + 1 does not contain the zero vector",
          "score": 0.8,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 6,
      "title": "Linear Combinations and Span",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.469,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "A linear combination of v1, ..., vk uses scalar coefficients",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The span of a set is the set of all its linear combinations",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The span is always a subspace",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "In R^3, the span of two non-multiple vectors is a plane through the origin",
          "score": 0.857,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "equation",
          "text": "A general linear combination: each vector is scaled by a coefficient and the results are added.",
          "score": 0.875,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 7,
      "title": "Linear Independence",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.677,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "v1, ..., vk are linearly independent if zero is only obtained with all coefficients zero",
          "score": 0.8,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Otherwise they are linearly dependent",
          "score": 0.667,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "If dependent, at least one vector is a combination of the others",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 8,
      "title": "Basis and Dimension",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.581,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "A basis of V is a linearly independent set that spans V",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Every basis of the same space has the same number of elements",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "This number is called the dimension",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The standard basis of R^n has n vectors, so dim R^n = n",
          "score": 0.75,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": 9,
      "title": "Exercise: Are (1, 2) and (3, 6) Independent?",
      "coverage": 1.0,
      "flagged": false,
      "novel_word_ratio": 0.649,
      "items": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The second vector is three times the first",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "So 3(1, 2) - (3, 6) = (0, 0) with coefficients not all zero",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Hence the vectors are linearly dependent",
          "score": 0.75,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "They span only a line, not the whole plane",
          "score": 1.0,
          "covered": true
        },
        {
          "kind": "equation",
          "text": "A nontrivial linear combination equals the zero vector, proving linear dependence.",
          "score": 0.875,
          "covered": true
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}